{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Hear, O Israel: YHWH our Elohim, YHWH is one.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the reason it mentioned three names — YHWH, our Elohim, YHWH — is that if it had said 'YHWH is one,' the nations of the world would have an opening to say that He said this out of fear of them, for He is called YHWH according to their understanding. Therefore it was necessary to say 'our Elohim,' that is, the Elohim of Israel. And likewise, if it had said 'YHWH our Elohim is one,' the nations of the world would have an opening to say: it is true that the Elohim of Israel alone is called one, but our Elohim, whom we call YHWH, is likewise one. Therefore it was necessary to say 'YHWH our Elohim, YHWH is one,' to indicate that YHWH, who is the Elohim of Israel, is one, and He alone is called 'YHWH is one,' and there is none besides Him, blessed be He, whose unity is complete from every side without any composition whatsoever, and in His unity He has no peer, neither among the supernal beings nor among the lower ones. And in the future it shall be so among all the nations, in the manner of what is written (Zechariah 14:9): ״ביום ההוא יהיה ה׳ אחד ושמו אחד״. And the reason it said 'our Elohim' and not 'your Elohim,' as it says everywhere in Deuteronomy (Deuteronomy 4:24): ״כי ה׳ אלהיך אש אכלה הוא״ — and likewise in all the passages where He speaks with Israel He mentions 'YHWH your Elohim' or 'YHWH your Elohim' (in the plural) — is because Moses wished to include himself within the unification." "<b>And in the name</b> of the master, R. Eliezer of Wormes, of blessed memory: YHWH — before the world; our Elohim — in the world; YHWH — after the world; one — in all the worlds." "<b>And you must</b> know that the matter of unification is what is called the service of the heart, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 11:13): ״ולעבדו בכל לבבכם״. And since a speaker may speak his words sometimes with intention and sometimes without intention, therefore it mentioned שמע, which is an expression that encompasses the hearing of the ear and the understanding of the heart — that is, he should direct his heart in his words regarding the matter of unification when he causes his ear to hear. And since the intention of the one who unifies is not revealed and publicized, whether his mouth and heart are equal or he mentions it with his lips while his heart is far from it — and it is impossible for a person to testify about his fellow in this matter — therefore you will find that the ע of שמע is large, and likewise the ד of אחד, to indicate that HaShem, blessed be He, is a witness to the matter, and He is the one who knows and bears witness to this. Therefore a person must be very careful regarding the matter of unification, to direct his heart and clear his mind of all thought, in the manner of what he said: he checked him in the chimney of the bellows until he directed his mind." "<b>And know</b> that the Garden of Eden and all its levels were created only for those who unify with intention, for whoever affirms the unity denies idolatry, and whoever affirms idolatry denies the unity. And Gehenna and its seven compartments were created only for idolaters. And because of this you will find in the perashah of Genesis 'garden' (גן) thirteen times, and in this perashah in its entirety you will find 'fire' (אש) thirteen times. And this is to inform you that whoever directs his intention in אחד, whose numerical value is 13, corresponding to the number of the thirteen attributes, is saved from the fire of Gehenna and merits the Garden of Eden and all its levels, which are thirteen — as they said, of blessed memory: R. Joshua ben Levi found R. Simeon sitting among thirteen rows of marble; and the allusion is to the thirteen levels of the Garden of Eden." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: שמע ישראל ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אחד — the Torah mentioned the verse of שמע after the Ten Commandments, to teach you that the utterance 'I' (אנכי) is the unification. R. Nathan says: from here is a reply to the sectarians who say there are two authorities — when the Holy One, blessed be He, stood upon Mount Sinai and said ״אנכי ה׳ אלהיך״, who protested against Him? And since this verse of שמע is a verse of unification, therefore Moses persisted in his language throughout Deuteronomy, to use the expression שמע, as he said (Deuteronomy 9:1): ״שמע ישראל אתה עובר היום״, and likewise (Deuteronomy 9:20): ״שמע ישראל אתם קרבים היום״, and likewise (Deuteronomy 9:27): ״הסכת ושמע ישראל״, and many others. And they said in the Midrash: שמע ישראל — when Jacob considered, perhaps there is a blemish among his sons as there was with Abraham and Isaac, he called his sons and said to them (Genesis 49:2): ״ושמעו אל ישראל אביכם״ — hear to whom Israel, your father, worships. They said to him: שמע ישראל ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אחד. And until now Israel follow the practice every day and say: שמע ישראל ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אחד — the שמע ישראל from the Cave of Machpelah: ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אחד. And they further interpreted: Moses said to Israel: you said at Sinai ״נעשה ונשמע״, and when you made the calf you lost ״נעשה״; and since you lost ״נעשה״, keep ״נשמע״; hence שמע ישראל. A parable — to what may the matter be compared? To a king who betrothed a lady with two pearls. She lost one. He said to her: guard the other. Thus they interpreted in the Midrash Rabbah." "<b>By way of</b> reason (sekhel): <i>Shema Yisrael</i>, etc. Know that the existent beings, in the upper and lower realms, are many, and from the standpoint of reason we are obligated to say that there is one true Being who emanated them and brought them into existence, for plurality derives from the power of unity. Just as the one precedes every number, so unity precedes every plurality. And since we see that the beings in the world are many, and it has been made clear to us that the power of plurality is drawn from the power of unity, this is a complete demonstration by way of reason that the essence of all existents and their cause is from the power of the One. And even though we find many things called by the name 'one,' this is not in the way of truth but only by way of transference and borrowing — for we find the Tabernacle, which Scripture called 'one,' and it is known that it contains many separate things and numerous parts. And because they were joined as one it was called 'one,' yet it can be disassembled from all its parts and they can return to what they were at first. And so it is written (Ezekiel 37:16): ״קח לך עץ אחד״ — 'take for yourself one stick.' And this is only by way of transference and borrowing, for in a single human being there are 248 limbs and he is composed of four elements that are destined to separate and each one to return to its element. How much more so regarding the lowly created beings, which are subject to change and division — for even among the supernal beings, which do not receive change and division, namely the separate intellectual substances, which are the souls and the angels, it is impossible for any one of them to be called by the name 'one.' For every angel has another like it or greater than it, and if so it is not 'one,' since there exists something comparable to it in its rank and power. And likewise the soul — there is another like it or above it in knowledge and subtlety of apprehension. Therefore it is impossible for any existent among all existents to be worthy of being called by the name 'one,' and not one among them is fit for this name at all, except the Master of all, blessed be He, who brought them into being after their absence. And He is the true One who precedes the many, and He is the Elohim in the heavens above and on the earth below, and He alone is the holy One who has no likeness, as it is said (Isaiah 40:25): ״ואל מי תדמיוני ואשוה יאמר קדוש״." "<b>By way of</b> the Kabbalah: <i>Shema Yisrael</i> — incline your ear and hear. In the verse <i>Shema</i> is the matter of the true unity, received and hidden among those who know the truth, in the secret of the unity of the ten sefirot, which we are obligated to unify all of them and to join everything as one, whether from below upward or from above downward. And the word <i>shema</i> is from the root of joining, in the manner of (1 Samuel 15:4): ״וישמע שאול״, and (Psalms 18:45): ״לשמע אוזן ישמעו לי״. <b>YHWH</b> — as (1 Chronicles 29:11): ״לך YHWH״, which includes the three uppermost ranks. <b>Eloheinu</b> — the greatness and the might. <b>YHWH</b> — the <i>tiferet</i>, until the completion of the structure, and afterwards <b>one</b>. And in the word <b>one</b> all ten are included: <i>aleph</i>, alluding to that which thought cannot grasp; <i>het</i>, to the eight things with Him; <i>dalet</i>, to the tenth, which is a great golden crown, and it is not fitting to separate it but rather to unify and join everything. And it is fitting to unify from below upward, because of the principle of <i>ma'alin ba-kodesh</i>, even though all is one in the service of unification. And regarding the <i>dalet</i>, which is the <i>malkhut</i>, the prophet said, peace be upon him (Isaiah 63:12): ״בוקע מים מפניהם לעשות לו שם עולם״, and we say in prayer: 'Your kingdom, YHWH our Elohim, your children beheld when You split the sea before Moses, the humble one — <i>this is my Elohim</i>.' And since He did not explicitly mention the quality of <i>malkhut</i> within the three names, therefore the Sages, of blessed memory, enacted to add and say <i>Blessed be the name of His glorious kingdom forever and ever</i> — that which Moses did not mention. And even though he did not mention it, they alluded to it in the letter <i>dalet</i>. And concerning this they offered a parable: a parable of a king's daughter who smelled the steam of a cooking-pot. If you were to say she has shame — do not say it; if you were to say she suffers — say it. What did her servants do? They brought it to her in secret. And the steam of a cooking-pot is something fragrant in its scent, restoring the soul. And the explanation of the parable regarding 'a king's daughter who smelled the steam of a cooking-pot': it is the <i>Shekhinah</i>, which sensed the praise of the tribes and the unification that they would perform for the great Name. And if you were to say her praise with their mouth — that just as He is One, so she is alone — she has shame; if you were to say she suffers when her glory is not unified with His glory — say it. And therefore Israel came, for they are the servants of the <i>Shekhinah</i>, and enacted to say <i>Blessed be the Name</i> in secret, which is the lower unification. And for this reason this praise of the <i>Shekhinah</i> was fixed in six words, like the verse <i>Shema</i> — these corresponding to the six extremities above and those corresponding to the six extremities below. And the king's daughter is the <i>dalet</i> of <i>echad</i>. And you already know that the letter <i>dalet</i> completes the count of ten. And you will find in <i>Sefer ha-Bahir</i>: What is <i>dalet</i>? A parable — to what may the matter be compared? To ten kings who were in one place, all of them rich, and one of them was rich but not like any of them; even though his wealth was great, he is called <i>dal</i> (poor) relative to the rich ones — end quote. And therefore the one who unifies must include all ten in the word <i>one</i>, because all of them are complete unity, for the ten reveal the power of the One who brought them into being, who encompasses the ten. And it is like the point, which encompasses the ten <i>yod</i>-like dimensions. How so? There is no point in the world that does not have length and breadth and depth, and for each of the three it has a beginning and a middle and an end — that makes nine; and the place makes ten, for a point cannot exist without place. If so, there are in the essence of the point ten together with the place. And likewise the <i>heh</i> that is unified, in which the <i>yod</i> is alluded to — for the <i>yod</i> with its point and the <i>heh</i> are three upper ranks, and the <i>vav</i> is six ranks drawn from them, making nine, and the <i>heh</i> in the manner of place makes ten. And the same applies to the <i>Kaddish</i>, in which one must include all ten, for <i>Amen, may His great name be blessed</i> is said upon the central line; <i>forever and ever and for all eternity</i> — these are five above, and likewise five below, for the central line it is, and all is included within it. And the <i>heh</i> in the <i>Kaddish</i>, in which one must include all ten — for the Supernal One is holy in <i>Hokhmah</i> and holy in <i>Binah</i> and holy in <i>Da'at</i>, which is the <i>dalet</i>; YHWH Tzevaot — the entire structure; <i>the whole earth is full of His glory</i> — in <i>Hokhmah</i>, as it is said (Proverbs 3:35): ״כבוד חכמים ינחלו״. And set it upon your heart that therefore the Men of the Great Assembly enacted in the Great <i>Kaddish</i> to begin with the word <i>Keter</i>." "<b>And know</b> that from the verse of <i>Shema</i> there emerges a Name of fourteen, and it is extracted from the third [word], and the three names — they are of <i>Hokhmah</i>, to make wise whoever mentions them, for he takes in the heavens a head. And the explanation of the verse: <b>YHWH</b> — it is <b>Eloheinu</b>, and the meaning of <b>YHWH one</b> is that <b>YHWH</b> which is <b>Eloheinu</b> is interchanged with <b>aleph-bet</b>, and that interchange is the meaning of <b>YHWH one</b>. Thus we have attained the indication of the unity of YHWH through the combination of His letters, and understand this. And according to what they said in the Midrash, the reason for <i>Blessed be the Name</i>, which was enacted to be said in secret, is because it is the praise of the ministering angels. And so they said in the Midrash Rabbah at the end of <i>Va'etḥanan</i> (Ezekiel 1:24): ״בעמדם תרפינה כנפיהם״ — when Israel come and stand silent, saying in a whisper <i>Blessed be the Name</i>, at that moment they [the angels] praise. And likewise Ezekiel said (Ezekiel 1:24): ״ואשמע אחרי״ — when I praised, the ministering angels praised <i>Blessed be the Name</i>. And why do they say it in a whisper? Rather, when Moses ascended on high he stole it from the ministering angels and taught it to Israel. Moses said to them: all the commandments that I have given you are from what I received from the Torah, but this that I am giving you is from what I heard from the ministering angels — and he taught it to Israel. Moses said to them: say it in a whisper. And therefore they say it publicly on the Day of Atonement, because they are like the ministering angels: they wear white, they do not eat and do not drink, there is no sin or iniquity in their hand, for the Holy One, blessed be He, pardons all their iniquities — end."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:52.898113412Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}